hello i'm george wilson and welcome to  the british council  in france today i'd like to welcome you  to our bilingual section open day the  bilingual section is a really integral  part of our offer in france we welcome  children from the age of four to 18  whose first language is english these  children speak really well  but they come here to have more practice  and to gain the literacy skills they'll  need as they grow  and develop so today i'm going to show  you just some of the projects that our  biblical students  take part in  so in this corner we're showing off to  our parents some of the books that our  early years students  so those who are four and five  study with us  you can see that the books we study are  designed to promote discussion  to get people  thinking about the stories but also  learning the phonic sounds that will  help them with their writing we also put  a heavy emphasis  on inclusion  in our stories and you can see how  there are lots of positive messages  in the books that we get our children to  study  and read together  so students in our  bilingual seven class are seven and  eight  and they study this book forgotten fairy  tales of kindness and courage  we do all kinds of work with the  literature that our children study  it might be  writing letters to the characters it  might be role play it might be creative  writing but we also get them to use  their hands you can see here a  representation  of one of the fairy tales in the book  i'll let you guess which one it is  in bilingual eight our students are  introduced to the concept of the  environment our programs put our core  skills at the heart of all of our  teaching these are things like  citizenship personal development and  generally being good 21st century  members of society really important to  that  is the environment you can see here  they've created a beautiful tree and  each leaf represents a tip  on how to support the environment and  tackle problems that are so important to  us all  by the time our students get to bible 9  they've got a good level they're really  getting a strong ability in reading and  writing  and so here we've got a challenge for  them  we get them to  learn poems by heart and recite them in  a competition with prizes it's great to  see how well they take on board the  challenge  and they kind of bring the poems to life  there are some brilliant quotes here i  think my favorite was i've learned to  read with my hands and my legs  bilingual 10 are delving into history  and history studies wouldn't be yet  complete without looking into the tutors  our students read a book called terrible  tudors  which is part of the horrible history  series  and at the end of all their studies we  get them to  recreate a banquet fit for king henry  viii or elizabeth the first  our teachers dress up  our students dress up  and  they recreate shakespeare plays dances  and all the details that really make a  good  tudor party  so we are trying to train good 21st  century students  students who care and give back to  society  in bilingual 11 our students study a  book called refugee boy by benjamin  zephaniah and we wanted to bring this  experience to life to get really  involved  in  making sure that refugees are well  welcomed  in france so with that in mind we  partnered with a charity called unia  this is a charity we do a lot of  outreach work for the association  welcomes refugees and migrants to france  and supports them in their educational  endeavors  part of that is providing a library of  reading material  our students designed posters  and interviewed  this woman from uni andy  um to learn all about the program the  posters then  spread the word and encourage people to  donate books  as you can see  the project was very successful we got  hundreds of books which have been very  much appreciated by those members of the  association  [Music]  so bilingual 12  had the opportunity this year to become  pen friends  with  members of our bilingual school in  madrid  we called the project connecting  cultures  and what was really nice about it was  how well the students took to the  project  they saw that this was a real  opportunity to experience culture from  different perspective  to learn about another country and also  just to have that good old-fashioned  connection with somebody who you don't  know  our valuable 13 students have been  studying that classic of literature  romeo and juliet by shakespeare so  to kind of end off their project  we asked them to perform for their  parents  the students used an abridged version of  the plane and collaborated across  different classes  in order to really bring the performance  to life you can see just some of the  photos here it was pretty incredible  and what's been really nice about the  whole thing is their enthusiasm bringing  a text that's so old to life with so  much vivacity is quite an achievement  huge congratulations to the students and  the teachers  [Music]  once our students finish the bilingual  13 they begin preparation for the igcse  in first language english  this is a qualification that will be  used by the learners to show their first  language ability in english when they  come to apply to universities  and also just have a really good sense  of their progression  and their achievement in following our  bilingual section  the exam takes two years to prepare and  in the first year there's still time  for them to take part in a public  speaking competition the learners really  took this to heart and did some  wonderful performances given in front of  her an invited audience some of the  bilingual students were the winners of  the competition and it was also a nice  opportunity for them to connect with  learners from our foreign language  section  once students finish their igcse and  they've got qualification in their  pockets they move on to thinking about  higher education now our learners have  the opportunity to follow a two-year  course known as university preparation  it prepares students to  take part in higher education  in english  our teachers are advisors so they're  able to give advice on the application  process  and the courses are designed to develop  the academic skills they'll need  for their future success  some of the things we do in the course  are quite innovative  we ask students to follow a mooc for  example an online course offered by a  university in this mooc they have the  opportunity to really connect with real  university teaching  at the end of their mooc which they  follow assiduously with um a diary  they produce poster presentations all  about the topics  now these poster presentations show the  sheer range of interests our learners  have  there's one about the sun one about the  marketing process  surrealism and dreams  so there's lots of different subjects  that interest our learners and this is a  chance for them to share their learning  and to show off their readiness for  university in english  [Music]  so our courses in the bilingual section  are mapped onto the british national  curriculum  that means that we've adapted the  program from the uk  to suit the developmental needs of  students in france  we have used a range of different  materials to support us in the same we  begin with phonics work work on  transcribing sounds into writing  and we use this nice series jolly  phonics then we move into the primary  section  where we use books  based on spelling  and books that develop  their general  english  abilities once they move into the  secondary section the books obviously  become slightly more complex  and we have some great material  ready for the learners this goes on into  it [ __ ]  and beyond that we use our own bespoke  materials for university preparation  one thing that's really important in our  section  is our range of literature the way in  which we integrate this into our course  courses  and the way in which we  use this to focus on different course  skills  these books are inclusive they're  dynamic and they're really at the heart  of what we can offer  first language english alerts  thank you for joining us today  on our bilingual open day it's been  great to show you around the british  council in france it's been great to  really show off the work that our  students have done  i'm really proud of their progress i'm  really proud of what we do here and i  hope you'll join us  in the future
 

